  <title>Images</title>

  <p><gui>+ Image</gui> asks for a JPEG or PNG file and puts it on the label.
  Dragged onto the canvas, it asks once you let go, and the image lands
  there. In ZPL it is <code>^GF</code>, the picture's dots written out in
  full.</p>

  <section id="dots">
    <title>From picture to dots</title>
    <p>A thermal printer prints only black dots, so the image is resized to
    its box and turned into a pattern of black and white dots. What the
    canvas shows is that pattern: exactly what is saved in the file and sent
    to the printer. Resizing the image makes the pattern again from the
    original picture, at the new size, so it never blurs from being resized
    twice.</p>
    <p>The picture is stretched to fill its box, and nothing keeps its
    proportions: dragging a side handle squeezes or widens it. To keep its
    shape, type both the <gui>Width</gui> and the <gui>Height</gui> in the
    same proportion as the picture, or drag a corner carefully.</p>
    <p>To use another file in the same place and size, select the image and
    press <gui>Replace Image…</gui> in the Properties panel.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="saved">
    <title>What the label file keeps</title>
    <p>The label file holds three things for each image: the dots the printer
    needs, a full-colour copy of the picture at the image's size, and where
    the original file was. When the label is opened again:</p>
    <list>
      <item><p>the dots are exactly the ones saved, so opening and saving a
      label never changes what it prints;</p></item>
      <item><p>a resize starts from the original file if it is still where
      it was, or else from the full-colour copy. Enlarging the image after
      its file has moved therefore softens it, since the copy is only as big
      as the image was. <gui>Replace Image…</gui> with the original file
      fixes that.</p></item>
    </list>
    <p>Images make label files large, and the printer receives the whole
    image every time the label prints: a 400 × 400 dot image adds about 50
    KB, and one twice as wide and tall about four times as much. A logo used
    on many labels can be stored on the printer once instead: see
    <link xref="stored-graphics"/>.</p>
  </section>

  <note>
    <p>White parts of an image are not white ink; they are simply not
    printed. Anything beneath an image prints through its white parts, and
    the canvas shows that. To keep something beneath from printing, clear
    its <gui>Print This Element</gui>. Images have no <gui>Reverse</gui>.</p>
  </note>
</page>
